Hi Friends,

Today, we, the Museum of the Palestinian People, are launching something new: Sumud Saturdays.

Every week, we'll spotlight one small piece of Palestinian history, culture, or memory. Some weeks it will be a literary spotlight. Other weeks, a village, a city, a tradition, an object, or a person who shaped Palestinian art and thought. The idea is simple. One story a week, so the history doesn't stay locked inside the museum, it lives in your feed and inbox too, passed down the same way it always has been, through our collective storytelling.

We're kicking things off with a book that has stayed on our shelf for a reason.



This week: Men in the Sun, by Ghassan Kanafani

Published in 1962, Men in the Sun follows three Palestinian refugees trying to reach Kuwait by hiding inside an empty water truck, chasing work and a future that has been closed off due to the Israeli occupation. It is short but unforgiving, and it remains one of the most widely read and taught works of Palestinian literature, assigned in classrooms and translated into more than a dozen languages.

Kanafani wrote it more than a decade before he was assassinated by a car bomb in Beirut in 1972. He was a novelist, journalist, and political figure, and his fiction remains one of the clearest windows into what displacement actually does to people. Not as an abstraction, but as a daily, physical weight carried across borders and checkpoints.

Men in the Sun asks a question that still sits at the center of Palestinian life today: what happens to people who are forced to leave home in search of a future that home can no longer offer them. We can't say this is a comfortable read, but it was never meant to be.

If you haven't read Men in the Sun yet, you can purchase it at Middle East Books & More . If you have already read it, we would love to hear what stayed with you.

Follow along every Saturday on Instagram, @museumpalestinianpeople, and in your inbox for the next story in the series.

We hope to see you at the museum soon!

Until next week and with you, always,

Leah

