Hi Dave,

If you ask most founders, they’ll say scheduling isn't a huge task. That's why it’s never top of the offload list.

But add up all the meeting requests you respond to, the back-and-forth to find a time that works, the rescheduling, the calendar checking you do before every commitment, and you’re looking at six hours a week, on average. (https://www.avoma.com/blog/time-spent-on-managing-meetings)

That’s over 300 hours a year, spent on something an assistant can handle completely, once they have access to your calendar.

We put together a guide that covers exactly that: step-by-step instructions for Google, Outlook, and Apple, including how to set the right permission levels so your assistant has everything they need and nothing they don't.

Want to take a look?
Yes! Take me to the article (https://www.timeetc.com/resources/how-to-use-a-virtual-assistant/how-to-share-access-to-your-calendar-with-an-assistant/)
All the best,


Barnaby
------
CEO and founder at Time etc (https://timeetc.com/ref/institute)
